  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nYet another round of showers and storms are moving northeast\nthrough Northeast Ohio early this morning with the third complex\nin about 36 hours across the region. The next round of shower\nand storm activity is already brewing in northern Indiana and\nsouthwestern Lower Michigan early this morning and will arrive\nin the western portion of the forecast area right around\ndaybreak. With the latest storms wiping out much of the\ninstability and a strong near surface stable, the upper bound of\nthis activity will be limited but an organized storm or two is\npossible with some marginally acceptable 0-3km shear around 30\nkts in the region. This activity will move southeast through the\nthe forecast area this morning. There could be potential for\nthis activity to become severe later in the morning (much like\nMonday) as the surface stability wanes with daytime heating.\n\nThe big show for today will be a line of thunderstorms that will\ndevelop with a shortwave along the front Minnesota and Iowa. \nThis line has great potential to be a healthy, severe MCS with\nample instability over the Midwest and great support with the\nshortwave aloft. The angle of this complex will largely take it\nsouthwest of the forecast area this afternoon and evening.\nHowever, the northeast extent of this line could reach into the\nforecast area and bring severe, damaging wind gusts to the area.\nIf this complex does become organized and ends up more into the\narea, then there could be potential for 75+ mph wind gusts. \n\nSeveral rounds of showers and thunderstorms are expected to move \nacross the region through tonight. With much of northern Ohio \nreceiving 1 to 2+ inches of rain since Sunday and another 1 to \n2 inches of rain with locally higher amounts expected today,\nhave issued a Flood Watch for a large portion of northern Ohio\nfrom 11 AM Tuesday to 8 AM Wednesday.\n\nOnce this larger MCS exits the region, this should allow for\nconvective activity to wane for Tuesday night into the first \npart of Wednesday as the surface front will be pushed well south\nof the area. The front will make a resurgence into region later\non Wednesday into Thursday as additional shortwaves in the \nnorthwest flow will move through the region and help support \nmore showers and storms. The potential for severe weather and \nflooding is becoming more limited on Wednesday with the front \nstarting so far south. With the front returning and lasting into\nThursday, there could be some low end severe and flooding \npotential with storms on Thursday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nWith the early week system slowing down, high pressure will be\ndelayed in entering the region until Friday. This will bring dry\nweather and less humidity into the region, giving some relief\nfrom the active and humid conditions from earlier in the week. A\ndelayed surface high will allow for dry weather to persist into\nSaturday, which will be a welcomed treat for the first half of\nthe weekend. Temperatures will fall back into the 70s with the\ncooler system overhead. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... \nThe upper ridge axis will push east on Saturday night and allow\nfor northwest flow to return to the Great Lakes region. A\nshortwave within the northwest flow will move through the region\nand support a low pressure system across the area. This will\nbring rain chances starting on Saturday night with the bulk of\nthe wet weather on Sunday and potentially into Monday. There is\nlow confidence on any severe weather or flooding potential at\nthis time.",
